---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import '../styles/marketing.css';

// Distinct from the homepage title on purpose: the homepage owns the product
// query ("self-hosted coding agents for Kubernetes"), while this pillar page
// targets the informational query. Identical titles would cannibalize both.
const title = 'Self-Hosted Coding Agents: A Practical Guide | GratefulAgents';
const description =
  'Why engineering teams self-host AI coding agents for data residency, IP protection, and cost control, and how GratefulAgents implements it on Kubernetes.';

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/self-hosted-coding-agents/#article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/self-hosted-coding-agents/',
    author: {'@type': 'Organization', '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/#organization'},
    publisher: {'@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/#organization'},
  },
  {
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/self-hosted-coding-agents/#breadcrumb',
    itemListElement: [
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 1,
        name: 'Home',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: 'Self-Hosted Coding Agents',
        item: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/self-hosted-coding-agents/',
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@type': 'HowTo',
    '@id': 'https://gratefulagents.dev/self-hosted-coding-agents/#howto',
    name: 'Run your first self-hosted coding agent on Kubernetes',
    description:
      'Bootstrap a local Kubernetes cluster with Kind and deploy GratefulAgents to run your first agent against a GitHub repository.',
    step: [
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        position: 1,
        name: 'Install prerequisites',
        text: 'Install Docker Desktop (or Docker Engine on Linux). The installer downloads Kind and the other cluster tooling for you.',
        url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        position: 2,
        name: 'Clone the repository and run the installer',
        text: 'Clone github.com/gratefulagents/gratefulagents, then run `make kind-install` to create a single-node Kind cluster and deploy the published release images.',
        url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        position: 3,
        name: 'Add your model credentials',
        text: 'Open the web dashboard, navigate to Settings → Credentials, and add your Claude or OpenAI API key. Your key is stored as a Kubernetes secret in your cluster.',
        url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        position: 4,
        name: 'Connect a repository',
        text: 'Create a GitHubRepository resource pointing at a repository you own, and configure a GitHub App webhook so issues and pull requests can trigger runs.',
        url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/docs/integrations/github/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        position: 5,
        name: 'Start a run and watch the trace',
        text: 'Open a GitHub issue or trigger a run from the dashboard. Follow live activity in the Agent Ops console and review the Jaeger trace once the run completes.',
        url: 'https://gratefulagents.dev/docs/runs/start-a-run/',
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base {title} {description} type="article" {structuredData}>
  <main id="main">
    <div class="mk-page">

      <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <ol class="mk-breadcrumb">
          <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
          <li><span aria-current="page">Self-Hosted Coding Agents</span></li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <div class="mk-hero">
        <p class="section-kicker">Architecture &amp; Evaluation Guide</p>
        <h1>Self-Hosted Coding Agents</h1>
        <p>
          AI coding agents are powerful. But where the agent <strong>executes</strong>, who holds
          the <strong>model credentials</strong>, and what <strong>audit trail</strong> it leaves
          behind matter as much as the task results. This guide covers what self-hosted coding agents
          are, why teams choose them, what the architecture requires, and how to evaluate your
          options.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/" class="btn btn-primary">
            Try GratefulAgents locally →
          </a>
          <a href="/differences/" class="btn btn-quiet">What’s different</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mk-layout-2col">
        <article class="mk-prose">

          <h2>What is a self-hosted coding agent?</h2>
          <p>
            A <em>coding agent</em> is an AI system that autonomously reads code, reasons about a
            task, calls tools (shell, editor, browser, APIs), and produces commits or pull requests.
            Unlike a copilot that autocompletes a line at a time, a coding agent manages a multi-step
            plan and iterates until the task is done—or until a human intervenes.
          </p>
          <p>
            A <em>self-hosted</em> coding agent is one where every part of that execution loop runs
            on infrastructure you control: your servers, your Kubernetes cluster, your network. The
            agent process, the sandboxed environment it uses, and the credentials it needs to call
            APIs all live inside your boundary. Code never leaves your network to reach the harness
            vendor's servers.
          </p>
          <p>
            This is different from <em>cloud-managed</em> coding agent harnesses, where you give
            the vendor access to your repositories and the agent executes on the vendor's
            infrastructure. Cloud services are convenient—you get a capable agent with zero
            infrastructure work—but they involve a different trust model.
          </p>

          <h2>Why teams self-host coding agents</h2>

          <h3>Data residency and regulatory compliance</h3>
          <p>
            Many engineering organizations operate under data-residency obligations: GDPR, financial
            services regulations, government contracting requirements, or internal policies that
            prohibit source code from leaving a defined network boundary. When an agent run executes
            on a cloud harness, the source files, prompts, and intermediate outputs all transit the
            vendor's network. Self-hosting removes the harness vendor from that path: the checkout,
            the sandbox, the tool calls, and the run history all stay on infrastructure you control.
          </p>
          <div class="mk-callout mk-callout-neutral">
            <strong>Be precise about the inference boundary.</strong> Self-hosting the harness is
            not the same as keeping every byte on your own hardware. Unless you point GratefulAgents
            at a model endpoint you host yourself, prompts and the repository context included in
            them are still sent to whichever provider you configure — Anthropic, OpenAI, OpenRouter,
            xAI, or Copilot — under that provider's terms. What self-hosting gives you is control
            over that boundary: you choose the provider, you hold the contract and the key, and you
            can see exactly what was sent in each run's trace. If your obligations forbid sending
            code to any third party, you need a self-hosted inference endpoint as well.
          </div>

          <h3>Intellectual property protection</h3>
          <p>
            Even where no regulation compels it, the business case for IP protection is real.
            Proprietary algorithms, unreleased product codebases, and trade-secret-bearing
            infrastructure code are high-value assets. Sending them to a third-party cloud harness
            introduces a trust dependency that many security teams are unwilling to accept.
            Self-hosting narrows that dependency to the model provider you choose: the repository,
            the working tree, and the run history stay on your hardware, processed by software you
            deployed and control.
          </p>

          <h3>Private dependencies and internal registries</h3>
          <p>
            Real codebases depend on private package registries, internal APIs, and
            network-restricted services. Cloud coding agents cannot reach resources behind a
            corporate firewall or VPN without complex tunnelling. A self-hosted agent runs inside
            your network and reaches all of those resources naturally, just like a developer laptop
            would.
          </p>

          <h3>Model cost control via your own contracts</h3>
          <p>
            Cloud agent harnesses typically bundle the model as part of the service. You pay the
            vendor's price for inference, with no visibility into the underlying model calls or
            token consumption. Self-hosting with a bring-your-own-key model lets you use your own
            AWS Bedrock, Anthropic, OpenAI, or OpenRouter contract, with the volume discounts and
            spending controls you've negotiated. Per-run cost and token observability then lets you
            optimize over time.
          </p>

          <h3>Auditability and reproducibility</h3>
          <p>
            When an autonomous agent makes a change, you need to be able to answer: what did it
            read? what tools did it call? which model responded, and with what? what decisions led
            to this commit? Cloud harnesses expose varying amounts of this—usually a high-level
            activity feed. A self-hosted harness that emits a full distributed trace gives you
            audit-grade records: every step, every tool call, every model prompt and response, every
            cost incurred. You can replay, audit, and compare across runs.
          </p>

          <h3>Reproducibility across environments</h3>
          <p>
            Self-hosting inside Kubernetes means the agent runtime is defined as infrastructure
            code—a Helm chart and CRD manifests. You can spin up an identical environment in a
            staging cluster, promote the same configuration to production, and version-control the
            whole setup. Cloud harnesses cannot be scripted this way.
          </p>

          <h2>What a self-hosted coding agent architecture requires</h2>
          <p>
            Running a coding agent yourself is more complex than signing up for a SaaS. The
            architecture needs to solve several hard problems:
          </p>

          <div class="mk-feature-grid">
            <div class="mk-feature-card">
              <h3>Isolated sandboxes</h3>
              <p>
                The agent needs a place to execute code and tools that is isolated from other runs
                and from your cluster's core services. Each run should get its own ephemeral
                environment with controlled network and filesystem access.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-feature-card">
              <h3>Credential management</h3>
              <p>
                The harness needs API keys for models, tokens for GitHub, keys for private
                registries. These must be stored securely, scoped per-project, and injected into
                the sandbox without being exposed in logs or traces.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-feature-card">
              <h3>Scheduling and lifecycle</h3>
              <p>
                Runs need to be queued, dispatched, monitored, retried on failure, and cleaned up
                when complete. A Kubernetes controller is the natural primitive here: it watches
                custom resources and drives them toward their desired state.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-feature-card">
              <h3>Trigger integrations</h3>
              <p>
                Agents need to be launched by real events—GitHub issues, PR comments, review
                feedback, Linear tickets, Slack messages, or cron schedules. Each trigger source
                needs a webhook receiver, event normalization, and security validation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-feature-card">
              <h3>An observability path</h3>
              <p>
                Every run should produce a structured trace you can query. Without this, debugging
                regressions in agent behaviour is guesswork. The trace should capture steps, tool
                calls, model responses, token counts, costs, and errors.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-feature-card">
              <h3>A human-in-the-loop surface</h3>
              <p>
                Autonomous agents make mistakes. You need a way to watch a run in progress, steer
                it with messages, stop it if it goes wrong, and retry from a checkpoint. A
                web-accessible dashboard is the minimum; mobile clients make this practical at odd
                hours.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2>How to evaluate self-hosted options</h2>
          <p>
            When choosing a self-hosted coding agent harness, the following dimensions are worth
            examining in depth:
          </p>

          <div class="mk-axis-list">
            <div class="mk-axis">
              <span class="mk-axis-label">Execution isolation</span>
              <span class="mk-axis-desc">How are individual runs isolated? Can a compromised agent process escape its sandbox?</span>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-axis">
              <span class="mk-axis-label">Credential scope</span>
              <span class="mk-axis-desc">Are secrets scoped per-project? Can one project's agent access another project's keys?</span>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-axis">
              <span class="mk-axis-label">Observability depth</span>
              <span class="mk-axis-desc">Can you trace every tool call and model exchange, not just a high-level summary?</span>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-axis">
              <span class="mk-axis-label">Model flexibility</span>
              <span class="mk-axis-desc">Can you switch models per-project or per-run, and use your own API keys?</span>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-axis">
              <span class="mk-axis-label">Trigger breadth</span>
              <span class="mk-axis-desc">Which event sources can trigger a run without manual intervention?</span>
            </div>
            <div class="mk-axis">
              <span class="mk-axis-label">Operational maturity</span>
              <span class="mk-axis-desc">Is there a Helm chart? Health probes? Upgrade path? How is state managed?</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            For how GratefulAgents sits against the cloud harnesses on these dimensions, see
            <a href="/differences/">the self-hosted alternative to Devin and GitHub Copilot</a>.
          </p>

          <h2>How GratefulAgents implements self-hosted coding agents</h2>
          <p>
            GratefulAgents is an open-source (AGPL-3.0) Kubernetes-native control plane for running
            AI coding agents against repositories inside your own cluster. Here is how it addresses
            each architectural requirement:
          </p>

          <h3>Kubernetes controller and CRDs</h3>
          <p>
            The core is a Kubernetes controller manager that watches custom resources:
            <code>AgentRun</code> (a single task execution),
            <code>Project</code> (a collection of settings and defaults for a repository group),
            <code>GitHubRepository</code>, <code>LinearProject</code>, <code>SlackAgent</code>,
            and <code>Cron</code>. Declaring a run is as simple as applying a manifest. The
            controller handles scheduling, dispatch, lifecycle, and cleanup.
          </p>

          <h3>Isolated sandboxes</h3>
          <p>
            Agent processes run in sandboxes provided by
            <a href="https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox" rel="noopener noreferrer">kubernetes-sigs/agent-sandbox</a>—
            a Kubernetes SIG project for isolated agent execution environments. Each run gets its
            own ephemeral pod with scoped access to secrets and no cross-contamination with other
            runs.
          </p>

          <h3>Bring-your-own model keys</h3>
          <p>
            GratefulAgents does not proxy model calls through its own infrastructure. You configure
            credentials for Claude, OpenAI, OpenRouter, Grok, or Copilot directly in your cluster.
            The agent uses those keys directly—no vendor intermediary, no usage data shared outside
            your cluster.
          </p>

          <h3>Trigger integrations</h3>
          <p>
            GitHub issues, PR comments, and review feedback trigger runs automatically via webhook
            receivers backed by <code>GitHubRepository</code> and <code>LinearProject</code> CRDs.
            A <code>SlackAgent</code> CRD enables Slack-triggered runs. <code>Cron</code> enables
            scheduled runs. All triggers are declared as Kubernetes resources—no external
            orchestration service required.
          </p>

          <h3>Per-run observability</h3>
          <p>
            Every run emits a distributed trace to an embedded Jaeger instance (deployed by the
            Helm chart). The Agent Ops console in the dashboard shows per-run cost, token counts,
            tool calls, subagent dependency graphs, compaction events, and errors. You can compare
            runs across models to understand performance and cost trade-offs.
          </p>

          <h3>Multi-platform clients</h3>
          <p>
            The dashboard is a web application. GratefulAgents also ships a desktop client for
            macOS Apple Silicon and Linux (AMD64/ARM64), and iOS and Android mobile clients. This
            means you can steer, stop, or review a run from wherever you are.
          </p>

          <h3>Agent modes and human oversight</h3>
          <p>
            Runs operate in three modes: <strong>interactive</strong> (you guide each step),
            <strong>plan</strong> (the agent produces a plan for your approval before acting), and
            <strong>autopilot</strong> (fully autonomous). You can switch modes mid-run, send
            steering messages, stop a run that has gone wrong, and retry from the last checkpoint.
            Agents open pull requests and iterate on review feedback—closing the loop from issue
            to merged code.
          </p>

          <h2>A path to your first self-hosted run</h2>
          <p>
            The fastest way to evaluate GratefulAgents is a local Kind cluster—no cloud account,
            no server, just Docker and fifteen minutes.
          </p>

          <ol class="mk-steps">
            <li>
              <div class="mk-step-body">
                <span class="mk-step-title">Install prerequisites</span>
                <span class="mk-step-desc">
                  Install Docker Desktop (or Docker Engine on Linux). The installer downloads
                  Kind and the rest of the cluster tooling for you. Full requirements are in
                  <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/">the getting-started guide</a>.
                </span>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div class="mk-step-body">
                <span class="mk-step-title">Clone and run the installer</span>
                <span class="mk-step-desc">
                  Clone the repository, then run <code>make kind-install</code>. This provisions a
                  single-node Kind cluster and deploys the GratefulAgents chart, including
                  PostgreSQL/pgvector, MinIO for object storage, and a Jaeger instance for traces.
                  It prints the local dashboard URL and the command to retrieve your admin password.
                </span>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div class="mk-step-body">
                <span class="mk-step-title">Add your model key</span>
                <span class="mk-step-desc">
                  Open the web dashboard, go to Settings → Credentials, and add your Anthropic
                  Claude or OpenAI key. The key is stored as a Kubernetes secret in your own
                  cluster and is used only to authenticate your calls to that provider.
                </span>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div class="mk-step-body">
                <span class="mk-step-title">Connect a repository</span>
                <span class="mk-step-desc">
                  Create a <code>GitHubRepository</code> resource pointing at a repository you
                  own. Configure a GitHub App webhook so issues and PRs can trigger runs.
                </span>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <div class="mk-step-body">
                <span class="mk-step-title">Start a run and watch the trace</span>
                <span class="mk-step-desc">
                  Open a GitHub issue or trigger a run from the dashboard. Follow the live
                  activity in the Agent Ops console and review the Jaeger trace once the run
                  completes.
                </span>
              </div>
            </li>
          </ol>

          <div class="mk-callout mk-callout-neutral">
            <strong>Note on current status:</strong> GratefulAgents is in early development. Bugs
            are expected. It is not yet suitable for production-critical workloads, has no
            documented high-availability configuration, and there is no managed hosting or support
            SLA. Treat it as early-adopter software and test it thoroughly before relying on it.
          </div>

          <h2>Where GratefulAgents sits</h2>
          <p>
            The best-known coding agents — Devin and GitHub Copilot's coding agent — are cloud
            services: the checkout, the agent process, and the model credentials all live on the
            vendor's infrastructure. GratefulAgents puts all three inside a cluster you run. The
            <a href="/differences/">differences page</a> lays the architecture out
            side by side.
          </p>

        </article>

        <aside>
          <div class="mk-sidebar-card">
            <h3>Try it now</h3>
            <p>
              Run your first self-hosted coding agent in under fifteen minutes using Kind on your
              local machine.
            </p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-kind/" class="btn btn-primary">
                Kind quick-start →
              </a>
              <a href="/docs/getting-started/self-hosting-k3s/" class="btn btn-quiet">
                k3s on a server
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mk-sidebar-card" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
            <h3>What’s different</h3>
            <p>How self-hosting changes the picture against Devin and GitHub Copilot’s coding agent.</p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a href="/differences/" class="btn btn-quiet">See the differences →</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mk-sidebar-card" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
            <h3>Open source</h3>
            <p>
              GratefulAgents is AGPL-3.0 licensed. Read the source, file issues, and contribute
              on GitHub.
            </p>
            <div class="cta-row">
              <a
                href="https://github.com/gratefulagents/gratefulagents"
                class="btn btn-quiet"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
              >
                View on GitHub ↗
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>

    </div>
  </main>
</Base>
